#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078337 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078494 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bcb5277-6d80-5cea-9688-ddb98f434093" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing and Solving a Characteristic Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "c47d5c55-80f0-5093-810b-bf9c718c5bb4" :question/math-academy-id "e-6587" :question/problem "Consider the equation $y''-3y'-4y = 0$ where $y=y(t).$ If $y = e^{\\lambda t}$ is a solution, then what is $\\lambda?$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y=e^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny'= \\lambda e^{\\lambda t},\\qquad y''= \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}-3\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}-4{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -4) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 -  3\\lambda - 4 = 0\n$$\n\nFactoring, we get\n\n$$\n(\\lambda -4) (\\lambda +1) = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots of the characteristic equation are $\\lambda = 4$ and $\\lambda =  -1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078338 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078495 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe7e5c4f-6ac0-58e8-8b95-fe5a2230341c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions of Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "2065790f-428d-5477-ac77-0c5d07853143" :question/math-academy-id "e-6588" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + \\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} - 2y =0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This is a second-order homogeneous ODE. So, we assume the solutions take the form $y=e^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\lambda e^{\\lambda x}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}-2{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the characteristic equation are $\\lambda = 1$ and $\\lambda = -2.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{x} + Be^{- 2 x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078339 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078496 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fcb5d818-acb0-53c7-90f9-328256600175" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Solutions of Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "1c7b0878-a386-5449-9ff4-c37c8cda1b92" :question/math-academy-id "e-6589" :question/problem "Given that $y = y(x)$ satisfies the equation $y''+7y'+6y = 0,$ which of the following could **not** be $y(x)?$\n\nI. $-3e^{-6x}$\n\nII. $2e^{-6x}-4e^{-x}$\n\nIII. $e^{-6x}+2e^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "This is a second-order homogeneous ODE. So, we assume the solutions take the form $y=e^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny'= \\lambda e^{\\lambda x},\\qquad y''= \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+7\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+6{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +6) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +6)(\\lambda +1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the characteristic equation are $\\lambda = -6$ and $\\lambda = - 1.$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{-6x} + Be^{- x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to determine whether each of the given functions takes the form shown above.\n\n- Function I takes the above form with $A=-3$ and $B=0.$ So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n\n- Function II takes the above form with $A=2$ and $B=-1.$ So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n\n- Function III does *not* take the above form. It contains $e^x,$ which is not included in the above form. So, it is *not* a solution to the given ODE.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421926 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078494 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bcb5277-6d80-5cea-9688-ddb98f434093" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing and Solving a Characteristic Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b840a02-6e20-5e04-b9ba-9bc1e18883f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-5$ or $7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421908} {:answer/id #uuid "41908330-21d9-59d9-a0b2-a53e1593c36e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-4$ or $-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421909} {:answer/id #uuid "4eb31420-e922-5f6e-bedb-6f1ece717ad4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ or $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421913} {:answer/id #uuid "7fa54804-bed0-557a-ba9a-e0d7bcc11265" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ or $7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421916} {:answer/id #uuid "912dd08d-5db0-5a08-ab81-5772fdca1e70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-3$ or $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421918}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7fa54804-bed0-557a-ba9a-e0d7bcc11265" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ or $7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421916} :answer-field/id #uuid "dafe6fcf-90f7-529a-8290-8d2af8b5a7f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "57b50761-0ebc-5773-b974-58926652c62b" :question/math-academy-id "q-129835" :question/problem "Consider the equation ${y}^{″}-12{y}^{′}+35y=0$ where $y=y(x).$ If $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ is a solution, then $\\lambda =$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-12\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+35{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +35) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +35 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda -5)(\\lambda -7)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =5$ or $\\lambda =7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421927 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078496 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fcb5d818-acb0-53c7-90f9-328256600175" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Solutions of Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47caa681-8680-5312-9d36-19b2868656e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{e}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421911} {:answer/id #uuid "4deac328-5c08-5937-b799-6f57cf09acfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421912} {:answer/id #uuid "646c2a9c-ee6b-59ef-abca-48eaa42e7b25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{e}^{-x}-7{e}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421914} {:answer/id #uuid "a500bf2b-0bcf-52aa-8621-3db9d47c8750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{6x}+{e}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421919} {:answer/id #uuid "f5419660-dfd9-56ab-8bf3-1a8501f74f1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-x}+{e}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421922}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a500bf2b-0bcf-52aa-8621-3db9d47c8750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{6x}+{e}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421919} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c296597-6c06-5bf8-aa85-d5015907d1ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab1d8df9-7551-58b4-b23c-3a36ae997ae9" :question/math-academy-id "q-130122" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}-6{y}^{′}-7y=0,$ which of the following **cannot** be $y(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-6\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}-7{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda -7) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda -7 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -7)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =-1$ or $\\lambda =7.$\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{-x}$ and ${e}^{7x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only ${e}^{6x}+{e}^{7x}$ is not a solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404496 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078494 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bcb5277-6d80-5cea-9688-ddb98f434093" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing and Solving a Characteristic Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c59fbbc-2dc0-562b-b432-ce3c7f59dad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-1$ or $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404498} {:answer/id #uuid "c01408ce-3680-5eb5-8582-5d15eb08699d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-4$ or $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404499} {:answer/id #uuid "0e45d023-bccd-58f6-9704-f559be5f8da8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-6$ or $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404500} {:answer/id #uuid "1b463397-33ab-5e64-907b-e8904f8a0935" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-3$ or $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404501} {:answer/id #uuid "d6e036bd-fcc3-5ff2-9e88-b80ab4086884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2$ or $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6e036bd-fcc3-5ff2-9e88-b80ab4086884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2$ or $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404502} :answer-field/id #uuid "117667a9-4c0a-559e-becf-2c255a4fa945" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404497}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a8bfd769-670f-5f6b-bec7-13134d36de0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-90706" :question/problem "Consider the equation ${y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-6y=0$ where $y=y(x).$ If $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ is a solution, then $\\lambda =$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}-6{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -6) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -6 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -3)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =-2$ or $\\lambda =3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421928 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078495 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe7e5c4f-6ac0-58e8-8b95-fe5a2230341c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions of Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43359a46-a31b-5979-b00d-e832c7aab359" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421910} {:answer/id #uuid "7d5b35fd-08b6-534e-aef6-d64720abed6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421915} {:answer/id #uuid "8b32650e-a838-5e42-b674-904b88326a9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{12x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421917} {:answer/id #uuid "b72954b9-0f27-5ed9-92fd-03be2ce3c9bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421920} {:answer/id #uuid "c1df491c-fb96-5d15-b8cb-b70016700d43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{3x}+B{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1df491c-fb96-5d15-b8cb-b70016700d43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{3x}+B{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421921} :answer-field/id #uuid "390cf455-f96c-59d6-8b71-3d6a053b53ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aecf7af1-6026-52e6-a339-611dfc1550ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-90715" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-7\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+12y=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-7\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+12{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +12) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +12=0.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -4)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =3$ or $\\lambda =4.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}+B{e}^{4x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357471 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078495 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe7e5c4f-6ac0-58e8-8b95-fe5a2230341c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions of Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02f538e8-ffd5-580a-b787-3529e176d8a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{4x}+B{e}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354683} {:answer/id #uuid "7577a907-a24b-5a1a-9252-dbbbb82ae05a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{14x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355696} {:answer/id #uuid "90cf1316-9d50-5a6d-896f-516e72559dcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-14x}+B{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355942} {:answer/id #uuid "a4ddbcdd-67e2-59df-87ae-7afbcd997f2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-7x}+B{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356128} {:answer/id #uuid "f2bcbb48-4d50-5e65-a98a-b2d2871a3b7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-5x}+B{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02f538e8-ffd5-580a-b787-3529e176d8a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{4x}+B{e}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354683} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c598865-38e3-50dc-b0e0-62772d08c731" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356991}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d5c18e84-2e36-57eb-8c9a-1a101f9f859c" :question/math-academy-id "q-90722" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-11\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+28y=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-11\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+28{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-11\\lambda +28) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-11\\lambda +28=0.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -7)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =4$ or $\\lambda =7.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}+B{e}^{7x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357473 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078496 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fcb5d818-acb0-53c7-90f9-328256600175" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Solutions of Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d8134cb-757e-5d42-9e52-e1be730cd855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{6x}+2{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354790} {:answer/id #uuid "3529b0ab-4f12-594c-a2f7-c68b64eaf2ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{6x}-8{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355139} {:answer/id #uuid "7d31d0c1-0468-5e56-877c-19e818ac36c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{6x-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355768} {:answer/id #uuid "d13b07b9-d726-574c-9d7e-e27a8da7875f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356495} {:answer/id #uuid "df457980-9f43-5760-a71d-325601bca0f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df457980-9f43-5760-a71d-325601bca0f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356618} :answer-field/id #uuid "019cf9ab-0c62-56d0-bba2-30385b4bf346" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356911}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "937412e4-d54f-5aaa-9272-a7d19f395a07" :question/math-academy-id "q-90729" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}-10{y}^{′}+24y=0,$ which of the following **cannot** be $y(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-10\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+24{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +24) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +24 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -6)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =4$ or $\\lambda =6.$\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{4x}$ and ${e}^{6x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $4-{e}^{6x}$ is not a solution."}]}
